    "q-36558": {
      "dom_id": "step-q36558",
      "name": "Question 5",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14205615/assets/92436599a8df12b4121e293adc861075d0bc8c7b31628922a7c2d8e2dad1cfef.png)\n\nWhich of the above could be graphs of odd functions?",
      "worked_solution": "Recall that the graph of an odd function must have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin.\n\n- Graph IV does not have rotational symmetry of order $2$ about the origin. So, it is **not** an odd function.\n\n- On the other hand, graphs I, II, and III do have rotational symmetry about the origin. If we rotate the graph ${180}^{∘}$ around the origin, we get the same picture. This means that graphs I, II, and III show odd functions.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14205615/assets/22c2e88d2e50c722c272e1a10902c58387f0ef00146be4838791c3258badaa5b.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
